## The idea

Two structures work together. A **Dictionary** finds any key in O(1). A **doubly linked list** keeps the entries in "most recently used" order, so the least recently used one is always at the back and can be removed in O(1). Each entry also remembers the time it expires; we check that only when someone reads it ("lazy expiry"). One lock around every public method makes it thread-safe.

## Step by step

1. Keep a dictionary from key → list node, and a linked list with the most recently used node at the front.
2. Get: look the key up. If missing → -1. If its expiry time has passed → remove it and return -1. Otherwise move it to the front and return the value.
3. Put: if the key exists, update value and expiry and move it to the front. Otherwise, if the cache is full, remove the node at the back (least recently used). Then add the new node at the front.
4. Two dummy nodes (head and tail) at the ends mean we never have to check for "first" or "last" — every real node always has a neighbour on both sides.
5. Wrap Get and Put in the same lock so two threads can never change the list at the same time.

## Worked example

**Input:** capacity 2; Put(1, 100, ttl 10) and Put(2, 200, ttl 10) at time 0

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| Put(1,100,10) | list: [1]; 1 expires at time 10 |
| Put(2,200,10) | list: [2, 1]; 2 expires at time 10 |
| Get(1) at time 0 | found, not expired → move 1 to front → list [1, 2]; returns 100 |
| Put(3,300,10) | full → remove the back (2) → list [3, 1] |
| Get(2) | not in the dictionary → -1 |
| Get(1) at time 10 | 10 >= expiry 10 → expired → remove it → -1 |

**Result:** 100, -1, -1 — key 2 was evicted as least recently used; key 1 expired exactly at its TTL

## Cost

Get and Put are O(1) time. Memory is O(capacity). Expired entries stay until someone reads them or they are evicted — that is the trade-off of lazy expiry.

## The code (C#, compiled and tested against every part of the question)

```csharp
// LRU cache where every entry has its own time-to-live (TTL).
public class LruTtlCache {
    // One node per cached entry. Prev/Next link it into the recency list.
    private class Node {
        public int Key;
        public int Value;
        public long ExpiresAt;     // the clock time at which this entry dies
        public Node Prev;
        public Node Next;
    }

    private readonly object _lock = new object();
    private readonly int _capacity;
    private readonly Func<long> _clock;                      // injected so tests can control time
    private readonly Dictionary<int, Node> _map = new Dictionary<int, Node>();
    private readonly Node _head = new Node();                // dummy: the most recently used side
    private readonly Node _tail = new Node();                // dummy: the least recently used side

    public LruTtlCache(int capacity, Func<long> clock = null) {
        if (capacity <= 0) throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(nameof(capacity));
        _capacity = capacity;
        if (clock != null) _clock = clock;
        else _clock = () => DateTimeOffset.UtcNow.ToUnixTimeMilliseconds();
        _head.Next = _tail;                                  // empty list: head <-> tail
        _tail.Prev = _head;
    }

    public int Count {
        get { lock (_lock) { return _map.Count; } }
    }

    public int Get(int key) {
        lock (_lock) {
            Node node;
            if (!_map.TryGetValue(key, out node)) return -1;  // never stored, or already removed

            if (_clock() >= node.ExpiresAt) {                 // expired: remove it now ("lazy expiry")
                RemoveNode(node);
                return -1;
            }
            MoveToFront(node);                                // a read counts as a use
            return node.Value;
        }
    }

    public void Put(int key, int value, long ttl) {
        lock (_lock) {
            long expiresAt = _clock() + ttl;

            Node existing;
            if (_map.TryGetValue(key, out existing)) {        // update: new value, new expiry
                existing.Value = value;
                existing.ExpiresAt = expiresAt;
                MoveToFront(existing);
                return;
            }

            if (_map.Count == _capacity) {                    // full: evict the least recently used
                RemoveNode(_tail.Prev);
            }

            Node node = new Node();
            node.Key = key;
            node.Value = value;
            node.ExpiresAt = expiresAt;
            _map[key] = node;
            AddAtFront(node);
        }
    }

    // ---- linked-list helpers (always called while holding the lock)

    private void AddAtFront(Node node) {
        node.Prev = _head;
        node.Next = _head.Next;
        _head.Next.Prev = node;
        _head.Next = node;
    }

    private void Unlink(Node node) {
        node.Prev.Next = node.Next;
        node.Next.Prev = node.Prev;
    }

    private void MoveToFront(Node node) {
        Unlink(node);
        AddAtFront(node);
    }

    private void RemoveNode(Node node) {
        Unlink(node);
        _map.Remove(node.Key);
    }
}
```

## Likely follow-up questions

**Q: Capacity 0? Re-put of an existing key? Get on a key that expired exactly now?**

Reject 0 at construction; update in place + renew; now == expiry is expired

**Q: Complexity of Get, Put and the sweep?**

O(1), O(1), O(budget) — and why the sweep is bounded

**Q: Where is the bottleneck?**

Not the algorithm — the single lock

**Q: Two threads call Get on different keys at once. What shared state do they touch?**

Both move nodes in one list: map + list invariant → one critical section

**Q: Lock or ConcurrentDictionary?**

Lock — the invariant spans two structures

**Q: 100× traffic, one hot key.**

Shard by hash; hot key still one shard → front cache or approximate recency

**Q: Memory pressure.**

Byte-weighted capacity; Resize down

**Q: What do you measure and alert on?**

Hit ratio (relative drop), evictions by reason, lock wait, size

**Q: The source behind it goes down.**

Serve stale with a bound, circuit breaker, collapse concurrent misses
